    body_en: "The act of naming looks at first like a neutral exercise in classification, but it is in fact an exercise of power that defines its object in a particular way. When a botanist assigns a scientific name to a newly found species, they become not merely a discoverer but an agent rearranging the world. The renaming of mountains and rivers that indigenous peoples had already named, carried out by Europeans arriving in the New World, is a blatant example of this power. Erasing an existing name and overlaying a new one was a symbolic conquest that reorganized memory of and claims to the land. Personal names, too, are not free of this dynamic. The custom by which a married woman takes her husband's surname long served as a linguistic device reaffirming the patriarchal order of the family. The recent rise, in some societies, of gender-neutral names or double surnames can be read as an attempt to resist this linguistic order. Naming diseases is equally political. Disease names bearing the name of a particular region produced stigma against that region and its residents, and it was for this reason that international health bodies came to recommend refraining from naming diseases after places or people. Naming brands and products, too, is a linguistic strategy meant to steer consumer perception in a particular direction. Thus a name is not a transparent tool pointing at a thing but a frame that predetermines how that thing is viewed. When we name something, we are already imposing one interpretation of it. The demand to change a name, therefore, should be taken not as a trivial play on words but as a serious attempt to redefine the world.",
